REGISTERED PREDICTIONS. Printed before anything is measured, and not rewritten afterwards.

  P1  the GEMM clock was sampled in the wrong state
        predicts  the under-load median lands in 1455-1515 MHz (the compute settle plateau), and differs from the same run's post-hoc idle sample by >5% on at least one of the two GEMMs
        a FAIL    the post-hoc sample was not the artefact. The 87.4%-versus-68.4% efficiency swing on one card is then unexplained, and no efficiency-at-clock figure in the study may be quoted.

  P2  the bandwidth side is already settled and re-measuring changes it by nothing
        predicts  read_reduce 4469.6, copy 4300.7, triad 4374.8, write 4682.4 GB/s, each within 0.5%; every pattern at 1980 -> 1980 MHz
        a FAIL    the memory settle does not hold, and every published GB/s is unstable at the size of the disagreement. The 2026-08-27 settle fix would then need re-opening, not just re-reporting.

  P3  the reduction shape still costs something
        predicts  read_stream > read_reduce (4469.6) and < the 4916.7 GB/s pin rate; the 2026-08-27 shape change was worth 1.7%, so 0-5%
        a FAIL    above the pin rate, the probe is broken -- dead-code elimination or a byte count -- and nothing from it may be used. Below read_reduce, ATen was not the limit and read_reduce stands as the read ruler.

  P4  the denominator question is the smaller half of the ruler problem
        predicts  the compute term's spread across sessions is >= 3.0x the triad-to-read denominator swing (9.9% against 2.2% in the committed files)
        a FAIL    the ruler CHOICE dominates its own instability, the re-baseline is about the denominator after all, and adopting the matched ruler is urgent rather than optional.

  P5  swinging the denominator changes no published row's bound
        predicts  0 rows of the canonical pool change memory/compute classification when the ceiling moves from triad to the matched read pattern (calibrate.py's own docstring: no cell changes anywhere in 4252.8-4656.9 GB/s)
        a FAIL    the 2.2% matters at row level. Every crossing in docs/FINDINGS.md is then quoted against a ruler that decides its own answer, and the re-baseline must be adopted before any of them is quoted again.

MDE. What a fresh calibration on this design could actually resolve.
  noise assumption: run-to-run timing spread MEASURED over the 26 published reports,
                    median 0.77%, worst 1.82%; 3 trials per pattern, both sides measured.
  effects under test: gate 2's reproduction tolerance 0.50%, gate 3's read-shape gain 1.70%.
  at the median spread 0.77%: MDE 1.76% against gate 2's 0.50%  CANNOT resolve it
  at the median spread 0.77%: MDE 1.76% against gate 3's 1.70%  CANNOT resolve it
  at the worst  spread 1.82%: MDE 4.16% against gate 2's 0.50%  CANNOT resolve it
  at the worst  spread 1.82%: MDE 4.16% against gate 3's 1.70%  CANNOT resolve it
  An MDE above the effect does not make a PASS wrong; it makes a FAIL uninformative.
  The spread is imported from KERNEL arms, which is the only measured one this repository
  has; a bandwidth pattern's own is plausibly tighter, so these are upper bounds and a
  design that clears them clears its own. Nobody has measured the pattern's spread, and
  until somebody does, gate 2's 0.5% tolerance is not known to be above this run's noise.
  Gates 4 and 5 are arithmetic over committed rows and have no timing noise to clear.

